 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Meruert Tolegen’s clothes occupy an intriguing place between romanticism and the enigmatic. At today’s presentation there were florals, beading, lace, and silhouettes that could have wandered out of Sense and Sensibility, if the Dashwood sisters had developed a taste for technical fabrics. While corsetry and sculpted construction gave the romance some backbone, engineered materials, including lace coated in a rubbery finish, added a harder, almost abstract note.</p><p>Tolegen’s approach is cumulative. Each collection builds on the previous one, carrying forward her interests in craft, handwork, and sculpting while pushing them somewhere new. This season, corsetry was often concealed within the pattern rather than displayed as decoration while sculpting shaped coats and dresses with varying degrees of rigidity. The little beads and appliquéd details, all handmade in-house, provided another thread back to her earliest work.</p><p>Yet for Tolegen romance comes with little interest in sweetness, or vulnerability. A dress can suggest an 18th-century nightgown while its construction feels thoroughly of the present; florals and feminine surfaces were countered by something more structured. “I want every look to contain that tension,” she said.</p><p>There is an inward quality to Tolegen’s work, which perhaps explains why it feels more moody than theatrical. She said it comes from her personality: “It’s kind of who I am internally.” She talked about the many facets of people, and the collection had that same elusive refusal to settle into a single state: quite sensibly, it didn’t seem interested in explaining itself all at once. Tolegen’s more private, self-contained approach feels refreshing; like their designer, the pieces left something unsaid, and that may be what made them intriguing.</p></div>",
